{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-178606",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to isolate the variable $y.$ To do this, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we add $5y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-y & =7-5y \\\\ 4x-y+5y & =7-5y+5y \\\\ 4x+4y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & =7 \\\\ 4x+4y-4x & =7-4x \\\\ 4y & =7-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y & =7-4x \\\\ \\frac{4y}{4} & =\\frac{7-4x}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}y}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\frac{7-4x}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{7-4x}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-178606\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>We need to isolate the variable <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"50\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D466 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>.</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> To do this, we treat <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"51\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.</p>\n<p>First, we add <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"52\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c35\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D466 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mi>y</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> to both sides of the equation:\n<mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" display=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"53\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math